<p><a class=cimage href="https://lars.ingebrigtsen.no/wp-content/uploads/2025/02/2025-02-17-11.jpeg"><img loading="lazy" decoding="async" src="https://lars.ingebrigtsen.no/wp-content/uploads/2025/02/2025-02-17-11.jpeg" alt="" width="840" height="299" class="alignnone size-full wp-image-110836" srcset="https://lars.ingebrigtsen.no/wp-content/uploads/2025/02/2025-02-17-11.jpeg 1374w, https://lars.ingebrigtsen.no/wp-content/uploads/2025/02/2025-02-17-11-300x107.jpeg 300w, https://lars.ingebrigtsen.no/wp-content/uploads/2025/02/2025-02-17-11-1024x365.jpeg 1024w, https://lars.ingebrigtsen.no/wp-content/uploads/2025/02/2025-02-17-11-768x274.jpeg 768w" sizes="auto, (max-width: 840px) 100vw, 840px" /></a></p>
<p>When writing blog posts, I use <a href="https://github.com/larsmagne/ewp" data-cached-time="2025-02-17T07:36:38" data-cached-image="https://lars.ingebrigtsen.no/wp-content/uploads/2025/02/ewpMzaUG4.png" onmouseenter="hoverLink(event)">ewp, an Emacs package to administrate WordPress.</a>  It offers an editing mode based on the revolutionary idea of just writing HTML.</p>
<p>Everything is cyclical in computing, so people move between <a href="https://claytonwramsey.com/blog/own-html/" data-cached-time="2025-02-17T07:36:42" data-cached-image="https://lars.ingebrigtsen.no/wp-content/uploads/2025/02/ewp8YpRZ9.png" onmouseenter="hoverLink(event)">writing things in raw HTML</a> and <a href="https://claytonwramsey.com/blog/no-html/" data-cached-time="2025-02-17T07:36:46" data-cached-image="https://lars.ingebrigtsen.no/wp-content/uploads/2025/02/ewpQm7teJ.png" onmouseenter="hoverLink(event)">using arcane and unholy systems</a>, mostly based on some Markdown dialect.  I understand the frustrations: It feels like there should be something that&#8217;s less annoying than using some WYSIWYG tool that invariably freaks out and ruins your post, or typing all that annoying HTML yourself, or using Markdown and then having to have some kind of build step.</p>
<p>In my opinion, Markdown is fine for writing README files, but if you&#8217;re writing blog posts, it just gets in the way.  A blog post is mainly just paragraphs like the one I&#8217;m typing (and you&#8217;re reading) now, which is just text with no markup.  Or there&#8217;s some slight formatting for emphasis or the like, but honestly, there&#8217;s not much difference between the HTML and Markdown versions for that.</p>
<p>Markdown is nice for headings and code snippets, but doesn&#8217;t really offer much useful for blog posts.  And the things that blog posts need, which is images/screenshots and links: Markdown doesn&#8217;t help you much there.</p>
<p><a class=cimage href="https://lars.ingebrigtsen.no/wp-content/uploads/2025/02/2025-02-17-2.jpeg"><img loading="lazy" decoding="async" src="https://lars.ingebrigtsen.no/wp-content/uploads/2025/02/2025-02-17-2.jpeg" alt="" width="840" height="55" class="alignnone size-full wp-image-110819" srcset="https://lars.ingebrigtsen.no/wp-content/uploads/2025/02/2025-02-17-2.jpeg 1082w, https://lars.ingebrigtsen.no/wp-content/uploads/2025/02/2025-02-17-2-300x20.jpeg 300w, https://lars.ingebrigtsen.no/wp-content/uploads/2025/02/2025-02-17-2-1024x68.jpeg 1024w, https://lars.ingebrigtsen.no/wp-content/uploads/2025/02/2025-02-17-2-768x51.jpeg 768w" sizes="auto, (max-width: 840px) 100vw, 840px" /></a></p>
<p>Is that really better than the HTML version?  And what, then if you need more stuff in the link?</p>
<p><a class=cimage href="https://lars.ingebrigtsen.no/wp-content/uploads/2025/02/2025-02-17-3.jpeg"><img loading="lazy" decoding="async" src="https://lars.ingebrigtsen.no/wp-content/uploads/2025/02/2025-02-17-3.jpeg" alt="" width="840" height="177" class="alignnone size-full wp-image-110820" srcset="https://lars.ingebrigtsen.no/wp-content/uploads/2025/02/2025-02-17-3.jpeg 1559w, https://lars.ingebrigtsen.no/wp-content/uploads/2025/02/2025-02-17-3-300x64.jpeg 300w, https://lars.ingebrigtsen.no/wp-content/uploads/2025/02/2025-02-17-3-1024x217.jpeg 1024w, https://lars.ingebrigtsen.no/wp-content/uploads/2025/02/2025-02-17-3-768x163.jpeg 768w, https://lars.ingebrigtsen.no/wp-content/uploads/2025/02/2025-02-17-3-1536x325.jpeg 1536w" sizes="auto, (max-width: 840px) 100vw, 840px" /></a></p>
<p>It just gets worse and worse &#8212; what if you need to put more data into the links?  The nice thing about HTML is that it&#8217;s well-formed and not very hacky &#8212; the more cruft you add to the HTML, the more unreadable it gets &#8212; but linearly.  Markdown makes the easy stuff trivial, and the difficult stuff worse.  (Here&#8217;s there the Greek chorus of &#8220;but you can just write HTML in Markdown&#8221; comes in, but that&#8217;s worse than just writing HTML in the first place.)</p>
<p>So: I write HTML, and Emacs takes care of displaying the images I&#8217;m linking to, so a blog post looks like this while I&#8217;m writing:</p>
<p><a class=cimage href="https://lars.ingebrigtsen.no/wp-content/uploads/2025/02/2025-02-17-4.jpeg"><img loading="lazy" decoding="async" src="https://lars.ingebrigtsen.no/wp-content/uploads/2025/02/2025-02-17-4.jpeg" alt="" width="840" height="584" class="alignnone size-full wp-image-110821" srcset="https://lars.ingebrigtsen.no/wp-content/uploads/2025/02/2025-02-17-4.jpeg 1380w, https://lars.ingebrigtsen.no/wp-content/uploads/2025/02/2025-02-17-4-300x209.jpeg 300w, https://lars.ingebrigtsen.no/wp-content/uploads/2025/02/2025-02-17-4-1024x712.jpeg 1024w, https://lars.ingebrigtsen.no/wp-content/uploads/2025/02/2025-02-17-4-768x534.jpeg 768w" sizes="auto, (max-width: 840px) 100vw, 840px" /></a></p>
<p>(To digress: I&#8217;ve noted over the years the many, many posts on HackerNews about statically generated blogs, and people have more fun spending time tinkering with their setups than actually writing blog posts, and that&#8217;s fine.  But I&#8217;ve noted that virtually none of these systems have a mechanism for dealing with images in a natural way &#8212; because that&#8217;s just kinda hard.  The nearest you get is &#8220;then you just create an S3 bucket and put the image there, and then you go to the AWS console to get the URL, and then you paste that into the Markdown here.  See?  PROBLEM SOLVED!!!&#8221;  That&#8217;s why blog posts from <a href="https://arne.me/blog/write-your-own-ssg" data-cached-time="2025-02-17T19:56:44" data-cached-image="https://lars.ingebrigtsen.no/wp-content/uploads/2025/02/ewpRSI1bf.png" onmouseenter="hoverLink(event)">all these people</a> (random example) are almost always just walls of text.)</p>
<p>Anyway, here&#8217;s my problem:</p>
<p><a class=cimage href="https://lars.ingebrigtsen.no/wp-content/uploads/2025/02/2025-02-17-5.jpeg"><img loading="lazy" decoding="async" src="https://lars.ingebrigtsen.no/wp-content/uploads/2025/02/2025-02-17-5.jpeg" alt="" width="840" height="299" class="alignnone size-full wp-image-110822" srcset="https://lars.ingebrigtsen.no/wp-content/uploads/2025/02/2025-02-17-5.jpeg 1392w, https://lars.ingebrigtsen.no/wp-content/uploads/2025/02/2025-02-17-5-300x107.jpeg 300w, https://lars.ingebrigtsen.no/wp-content/uploads/2025/02/2025-02-17-5-1024x365.jpeg 1024w, https://lars.ingebrigtsen.no/wp-content/uploads/2025/02/2025-02-17-5-768x274.jpeg 768w" sizes="auto, (max-width: 840px) 100vw, 840px" /></a></p>
<p>YIKES!  WHAT THE&#8230;  Yes, I hear you.</p>
<p>To protect myself a bit against link rot, <a screenshot=true href="https://lars.ingebrigtsen.no/2020/04/06/the-campaign-against-link-rot/">ewp screenshots everything I link to</a> automatically.  So on the blog, you can just hover over a link to see (and read, if you want to) what I was linking to at the time, and that will survive as long as my blog survives (while most of the things I&#8217;m linking to disappear, apparently).</p>
<p>But that means that I have to stash that data somewhere, and I stashed it in the links, which means that the HTML then becomes unreadable.</p>
<p>This is Emacs, however.  What about just hiding all that junk?</p>
<p><a class=cimage href="https://lars.ingebrigtsen.no/wp-content/uploads/2025/02/2025-02-17-6.jpeg"><img loading="lazy" decoding="async" src="https://lars.ingebrigtsen.no/wp-content/uploads/2025/02/2025-02-17-6.jpeg" alt="" width="840" height="49" class="alignnone size-full wp-image-110823" srcset="https://lars.ingebrigtsen.no/wp-content/uploads/2025/02/2025-02-17-6.jpeg 1347w, https://lars.ingebrigtsen.no/wp-content/uploads/2025/02/2025-02-17-6-300x18.jpeg 300w, https://lars.ingebrigtsen.no/wp-content/uploads/2025/02/2025-02-17-6-1024x60.jpeg 1024w, https://lars.ingebrigtsen.no/wp-content/uploads/2025/02/2025-02-17-6-768x45.jpeg 768w" sizes="auto, (max-width: 840px) 100vw, 840px" /></a></p>
<p>Yes, that&#8217;s the same paragraph with the links hidden.  And if I want to edit the links themselves, I can just hit <tt>TAB</tt> on the bracket:</p>
<p><a class=cimage href="https://lars.ingebrigtsen.no/wp-content/uploads/2025/02/2025-02-17-7.jpeg"><img loading="lazy" decoding="async" src="https://lars.ingebrigtsen.no/wp-content/uploads/2025/02/2025-02-17-7.jpeg" alt="" width="840" height="142" class="alignnone size-full wp-image-110824" srcset="https://lars.ingebrigtsen.no/wp-content/uploads/2025/02/2025-02-17-7.jpeg 1382w, https://lars.ingebrigtsen.no/wp-content/uploads/2025/02/2025-02-17-7-300x51.jpeg 300w, https://lars.ingebrigtsen.no/wp-content/uploads/2025/02/2025-02-17-7-1024x173.jpeg 1024w, https://lars.ingebrigtsen.no/wp-content/uploads/2025/02/2025-02-17-7-768x130.jpeg 768w" sizes="auto, (max-width: 840px) 100vw, 840px" /></a></p>
<p>And <tt>TAB</tt> again to hide:</p>
<p><a class=cimage href="https://lars.ingebrigtsen.no/wp-content/uploads/2025/02/2025-02-17-8.jpeg"><img loading="lazy" decoding="async" src="https://lars.ingebrigtsen.no/wp-content/uploads/2025/02/2025-02-17-8.jpeg" alt="" width="840" height="57" class="alignnone size-full wp-image-110825" srcset="https://lars.ingebrigtsen.no/wp-content/uploads/2025/02/2025-02-17-8.jpeg 1365w, https://lars.ingebrigtsen.no/wp-content/uploads/2025/02/2025-02-17-8-300x20.jpeg 300w, https://lars.ingebrigtsen.no/wp-content/uploads/2025/02/2025-02-17-8-1024x70.jpeg 1024w, https://lars.ingebrigtsen.no/wp-content/uploads/2025/02/2025-02-17-8-768x52.jpeg 768w" sizes="auto, (max-width: 840px) 100vw, 840px" /></a></p>
<p>Note that the links and stuff are still present in the Emacs buffer, so the normal Emacs autosave functions work perfectly, and there&#8217;s no danger of losing any data.</p>
<p>Similarly, the image HTML in WordPress can be pretty messy:</p>
<p><a class=cimage href="https://lars.ingebrigtsen.no/wp-content/uploads/2025/02/2025-02-17-9.jpeg"><img loading="lazy" decoding="async" src="https://lars.ingebrigtsen.no/wp-content/uploads/2025/02/2025-02-17-9.jpeg" alt="" width="840" height="143" class="alignnone size-full wp-image-110831" srcset="https://lars.ingebrigtsen.no/wp-content/uploads/2025/02/2025-02-17-9.jpeg 1369w, https://lars.ingebrigtsen.no/wp-content/uploads/2025/02/2025-02-17-9-300x51.jpeg 300w, https://lars.ingebrigtsen.no/wp-content/uploads/2025/02/2025-02-17-9-1024x175.jpeg 1024w, https://lars.ingebrigtsen.no/wp-content/uploads/2025/02/2025-02-17-9-768x131.jpeg 768w" sizes="auto, (max-width: 840px) 100vw, 840px" /></a></p>
<p>Because images have extra classes with their IDs, and you can click on images to get the full sizes, so they&#8217;re (almost always) wrapped in an &lt;a&gt;.  Now, when writing articles, Emacs displays the images instead of the HTML, so we don&#8217;t see all that cruft anyway, but when editing image heavy articles, it can take some time to fetch the images, and we don&#8217;t want to be staring at junk like that while waiting for the images to arrive.</p>
<p>So let&#8217;s hide them like this:</p>
<p><a class=cimage href="https://lars.ingebrigtsen.no/wp-content/uploads/2025/02/2025-02-17-10.jpeg"><img loading="lazy" decoding="async" src="https://lars.ingebrigtsen.no/wp-content/uploads/2025/02/2025-02-17-10.jpeg" alt="" width="54" height="77" class="alignnone size-full wp-image-110832" /></a><br />
And <tt>TAB</tt> can be used to cycle through the three different forms:</p>
<p><video autoplay loop muted><source src="https://lars.ingebrigtsen.no/wp-content/uploads/2025/02/Screencast-from-2025-02-17-19-05-12.mp4" poster="https://lars.ingebrigtsen.no/wp-content/uploads/2025/02/poster2KwhO6001.jpg"  type="video/mp4"></video></p>
<p>I think that looks kinda pleasant to work with&#8230;</p>
<p>Anyway, I think that&#8217;s as far as I want to go with hiding the HTML-ness of things.  I mean, the temptation here is to start going in a more WYSIWYG direction, and translating &lt;b&gt;&#8230;&lt;/b&gt; into bold text and all that sort of stuff, but&#8230;  I&#8217;m more comfortable just looking at the tags?</p>
<p>So there you go: In the &#8220;just write HTML/no don&#8217;t write HTML&#8221; wars, I&#8217;m on &#8220;just write HTML but have the editor hide some of the worst of the cruft&#8221; tip.</p>
